Joules per frame for ResNet50 across CPU, iGPU and NPU -- the first per-model energy
numbers in this repo, and the answer is emphatic: **the NPU delivers 19.6 inferences per
joule against the iGPU's 3.3 and the CPU's 1.7**, i.e. **11.3x the CPU and 5.9x the iGPU**,
while also being 2.80x and 1.34x faster respectively.
Desktop 2 (Ryzen 7 8700G / Phoenix XDNA1 / Radeon 780M), DESKTOP-CBL5NUA, 2026-09-09,
22:24-22:38. Six power phases in one sitting, 60 s sampled each; throughput re-measured
separately on a clean host (see METHODOLOGY -- the first throughput pass was contaminated
and is retracted).

WHY THIS EXISTS
===============
`power_rapl_bf16_gemm_npu_vs_cpu.log` measured the first watt and found the NPU 4.45x more
energy-efficient on a bf16 GEMM, but explicitly could not answer the question the backlog
actually asks: that was joules per GEMM on a synthetic shape, not joules per FRAME on a
real model, and it had no iGPU leg at all. This closes both gaps for one model.

The dtype confound is the reason phase E exists. The NPU runs XINT8 and the CPU/iGPU run
FP32, because that is what each can actually deploy -- so a naive three-way comparison
cannot tell "the NPU is efficient" from "int8 is cheap". Phase E runs the CPU on the SAME
XINT8 artifact the NPU uses, which separates them.

WHAT WAS RUN
============
`tools/power_probe.py` sampling RAPL package and per-core power (see the GEMM log for the
unit calibration -- `Power` is milliwatts, MEASURED by burn) while `tools/session_hold.py`
holds one inference session busy. `session_hold.py` gained an `--ep {npu,cpu,dml}` flag
for this; it previously hardcoded NPU.

  A idle -> B CPU fp32 -> C iGPU/DML fp32 -> D NPU xint8 -> E CPU xint8 -> F idle again

Models: `resnet50_fp32.onnx` (B, C) and `resnet50_xint8_adaround.onnx` (D, E), single
stream, batch 1, 224x224. The NPU phase compiled fresh (`--fresh`) before sampling.

MEDIANS, NOT MEANS, and the reason matters
==========================================
Phase F's package MEAN is 44.815 W against phase A's 41.513 -- an 8% apparent drift that
would undermine every delta below. It is an artefact: F's max is 74.029 W and its core-sum
max 59.108 W, i.e. one transient burst from the peer process inside an otherwise idle
window. The MEDIANS are 41.304 W (A) and 41.633 W (F) -- **0.8% apart**, which is the same
stability the GEMM sitting showed. Every figure below therefore uses per-phase medians,
and the idle baseline is the median of A and F: **41.469 W package, 24.901 W cores,
16.568 W residual**.

RESULT
======
  phase                 package    cores sum   residual    fps
  ----------------------------------------------------------------
  A idle                 41.304 W    25.078 W   16.226 W     --
  B CPU fp32             82.495 W    63.551 W   18.944 W    70.9
  C iGPU (DML) fp32      86.302 W    22.648 W   63.654 W   148.0
  D NPU xint8            51.591 W    26.061 W   25.530 W   198.2
  E CPU xint8 (control)  73.714 W    59.077 W   14.636 W    30.1
  F idle again           41.633 W    24.724 W   16.909 W     --

Deltas over the idle baseline, and the attribution is clean on all three providers:

  arm              d package   d cores    d residual
  ------------------------------------------------------
  CPU fp32          41.026 W   +38.650 W    +2.376 W
  iGPU fp32         44.834 W    -2.253 W   +47.086 W
  NPU xint8         10.122 W    +1.160 W    +8.962 W
  CPU xint8         32.245 W   +34.176 W    -1.932 W

Each provider lights up exactly where it lives. The CPU arms put nearly all of their draw
in the cores. **The iGPU's cores go BELOW idle** (-2.25 W, the CPU is mostly waiting on it)
while the residual takes +47.09 W -- the 780M is in the SoC/uncore domain, not the core
domain. The NPU takes +8.96 W of residual for +1.16 W of cores, the same signature the
bf16 GEMM sitting found. Nothing here required assuming which silicon block was drawing.

ENERGY PER FRAME
================
  arm                  J/frame marginal   J/frame total   frames per marginal joule
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  CPU fp32                  0.57865          1.1635                 1.73
  iGPU (DML) fp32           0.30293          0.5831                 3.30
  **NPU xint8**             **0.05107**      **0.2603**            **19.58**
  CPU xint8 (control)       1.07128          2.4490                 0.93

  NPU vs CPU fp32   : 11.33x more efficient (marginal), 4.47x (total), 2.80x faster
  NPU vs iGPU       :  5.93x more efficient (marginal), 2.24x (total), 1.34x faster
  NPU vs CPU xint8  : 20.98x more efficient (marginal),               6.58x faster

**THE DTYPE CONTROL IS DECISIVE, AND IT RULES OUT THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE EXPLANATION.**
If the NPU's advantage were really "int8 is cheaper arithmetic", the CPU running the same
XINT8 artifact should have moved toward the NPU. It moves the other way, hard: CPU xint8 is
**slower than CPU fp32** (30.1 vs 70.9 fps) and **1.85x LESS energy-efficient per frame**
(1.071 vs 0.579 J). ONNX Runtime's QDQ int8 path on this CPU is a pessimization, not an
optimization. So the NPU's 11.3x is attributable to the hardware executing a quantized
graph natively, not to quantization itself -- the two were confounded before this control
and are not now.

Secondary finding: **the iGPU beats the CPU on energy** (3.30 vs 1.73 frames/J) while
drawing the most package power of any arm (86.3 W, higher even than the all-core CPU
phase). It buys that back with 2.09x the CPU's throughput. It still loses to the NPU by
5.93x. So on this chip the ordering for inference energy is NPU >> iGPU > CPU, and it is
not close at the top.

METHODOLOGY -- a contaminated measurement, caught and retracted
==============================================================
The study script measured throughput immediately after each power phase, in a separate
short run. **Every one of those four figures was roughly half its true value** and all
four are retracted:

  arm          contaminated   clean    factor
  CPU fp32         37.5       70.9      1.89x
  iGPU fp32        72.3      148.0      2.05x
  NPU xint8        90.6      198.2      2.19x
  CPU xint8        18.1       30.1      1.66x

Cause: `power_probe.py` terminates its `--command` workload when sampling ends, and the
study started the throughput run immediately afterwards. The 95 s hold had not exited yet,
so two sessions were competing for one provider through the whole throughput window.

It was caught by a consistency check rather than by luck: the NPU's contaminated 90.6 fps
disagreed with this repo's own documented ResNet50 NPU latency (5.27 ms, i.e. ~190 fps),
and a clean re-run returned **198.2 fps with near-zero variance** (samples 197.7-198.7
across 12 intervals). The other three were then re-taken the same way, each on an
otherwise-idle host with an 8 s settle between arms; all four are stable to under 1%.

**The POWER phases are not retracted, but "each ran exactly one workload" was never
actually true (added 2026-09-10).** `power_probe.py` ended its `--command` workload with
`proc.terminate()`, which on Windows kills only the `cmd.exe` wrapper `shell=True`
creates, not the workload underneath it -- confirmed directly while investigating the
same tool's contamination of `power_rapl_bisenetv2_joules_per_frame.log`'s power phases.
Here each phase's inline throughput check (this section) ran immediately after that
phase's own probe and itself held the provider for ~25 s -- long enough to occupy most of
the orphaned workload's remaining ~32 s lifetime before the NEXT phase's probe started, so
this study's power phases were shielded from the bug mostly by that lucky sequencing, not
by isolation. The residual bleed this leaves (a few seconds at most into the next phase)
is far smaller than what BiSeNetV2's fully-separated design exposed, and is consistent
with `phase F`'s own outlier max (74.0 W, matching phase E's load) rather than solely the
peer process blamed above -- both may have contributed. Fixed in `tools/power_probe.py`
(tree-kill via `taskkill /F /T`); no re-measurement of this log was judged necessary given
the small bound on the residual and that medians (not means) already carry this study's
headline numbers.

CONTENTION
==========
The peer process (PID 22396) was live throughout, taking 25,601.0 -> 26,097.0 CPU-seconds
across the study, i.e. ~0.7 of 16 logical cores. It is present in all six phases including
both idles, so it cancels from the deltas; the A-vs-F median agreement of 0.8% is the
evidence its load was steady rather than drifting. It is also the source of the burst that
made F's mean unusable, which is why medians are used. `xrt-smi` reported no hardware
contexts before or after.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH
============================
- **One model, one batch size, one stream.** ResNet50 at batch 1 is the NPU's best case --
  a small INT8 CNN is exactly what this hardware is for. A model the EP partitions badly,
  or one whose frames are large enough to be bandwidth-bound, could land anywhere.
- **Not sustained thermal steady state.** 60 s windows, not the 15-30 min the backlog item
  asks for. Nothing here shows what happens once the package heats and clocks settle.
- **Throughput and power come from different windows** (same configuration, minutes
  apart). They were not captured simultaneously, so a J/frame figure is a product of two
  separately-measured quantities rather than one integrated measurement.
- **The NPU residual is an upper bound**, not an isolate: package minus cores includes
  uncore, SoC, memory controller and NPU together.
- **No accuracy axis.** XINT8+AdaRound ResNet50 is 79.80% top-1 against the FP32 model's
  higher figure; this log compares energy at fixed *work*, not at fixed accuracy, and a
  frame is not worth the same on both.
- **Idle is not free**, and it dominates the total column: the machine burns ~41.5 W doing
  nothing (with a peer taking ~0.7 cores), which is why marginal and total differ by so
  much. On a battery-relevant platform the idle floor would be far lower and the total
  column would move toward the marginal one.

